      "writtenOn": "2020-06-25T23:42:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e IIUC, \"use_vendor: true\" makes the APEX to use vndk libraries instead of core variant libraries, so it\u0027s /system-APEX using a \".vendor\" variant library and such \".vendor\" variant library is installed in /system?\n\nYes and No. ;-)\n\n\"use_vendor:true\" APEX uses vendor variants of libs including VNDK libs and LLNDK stubs. But VNDK libs are not individually installed in /system/lib, but packaged in the apex itself. But LLNDK stubs are external dependencies and should be listed as \"requiredDeps\". But, in this case, because \"use_vendor:true\" APEX is still a system-side APEX, we ignore \".vendor\" suffix so that these libs are properly installed when the apex is installed.\n\nAnd, to be precise, VNDK libs are further split into apex-specific variants. So the module names will look like \"module.myapex\". For example, when libc++ is used by \"com.android.foo\" apex, the apex variant of libc++ is emitted to .mk file as \"libc++.com.android.foo\".",
      "parentUuid": "8ca59018_6d35bf44",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2139,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 2140,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "efb184e499edd2f0a1f717ab68e2052eb597adab",
